WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.690
Steve Rogers, the World War II hero and perennial
Avenger known as Captain America, has been

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:10.720
a cultural icon since he was famously introduced
punching Adolf Hitler in the face on the cover

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.960
of Captain America Comics #1.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.500
While you might already know about his decades
spent as an icicle or his frenemy relationship

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.740
with Iron Man, there's plenty of lesser-known
details about the Sentinel of Liberty.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.020
Here is the untold truth of Captain America.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.720
"Before we get started, does anyone want to
get out?"

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.340
He trains every Avenger

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.791
Captain America is among the finest hand-to-hand
combatants in the Marvel Universe, thanks

00:00:39.791 --> 00:00:44.260
to his vast experience, training, and enhanced
strength and reflexes.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.740
As such, he trains just about every hero who
has come through the Avengers' revolving door.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:54.160
Among his more interesting students: the invincible
Iron Man, She-Hulk, Hawkeye, and even Mary-Jane

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:54.680
Watson.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.360
Maybe he should be called Drill Sergeant America.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.580
"Your fighting style is to tackle people or
blast them.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.880
If I had to guess, I'd say you've never been
in an actual fight in your life."

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.500
Multiple Buckys

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.560
Almost as well-known as Cap himself is his
original sidekick Bucky Barnes.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:17.020
Barnes was a mascot at the military base where
Rogers was stationed in the early days of

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.560
World War II who was taken under the Captain's
wings.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.340
Bucky was presumed dead along with Cap in
the fateful plane crash that led to Cap's

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:28.820
hibernation, although the young sidekick would
return decades later as the Winter Soldier.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.360
"Bucky?"

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.240
"Who the hell is Bucky?"

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:37.060
Rogers would eventually pick up another young
sidekick in the modern era, Rick Jones.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.780
Rick Jones was directly involved in the accident
which created the Hulk, and it was his distress

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.360
call which initially caused the Avengers to
form.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:49.540
Having been taken in by the team as an honorary
member, Jones answered the call when Cap found

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:53.939
himself in need of another partner, undergoing
combat training and even, for a time, wearing

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.220
Bucky's old uniform.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:59.480
The partnership didn't last long, but Rick
Jones would continue to be a sidekick for

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.120
just about everyone in the Marvel Universe.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.640
Replaced by Black Panther

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:09.440
In Avengers #47, Cap decides to take a leave
of absence from the team to set off on his

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.760
own adventures — a journey that would bring
him to Wakanda, where he would encounter perhaps

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.500
his only viable replacement in Black Panther.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:22.800
"Tell me Captain America, is it really your
shield?"

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.120
After helping Cap to foil a plot by Baron
Zemo to destroy the United States using advanced

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.060
weaponry, T'Challa accompanies Cap back to
America, mulling over his suggestion to join

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.520
the Avengers as Cap's replacement.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.200
T'Challa quickly proves his worth in a fight
against the Grim Reaper, and the grateful

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.000
team makes T'Challa a full-fledged member.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.710
After that, Cap would only make cameo appearances
in Avengers comics until returning to the

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.140
team a full three years later.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.280
The Falcon and the Red Skull

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.460
Sam Wilson, the Falcon, has been Cap's staunchest
friend and ally over the years.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.840
Their formal partnership began way back in
1971's Captain America #133.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.760
But strangely, their long association never
would have happened if not for one of the

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.880
Red Skull's many evil plots to kill Captain
America.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:16.550
In Captain America #117, Steve Rogers is banished
to a mysterious island after getting his body

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:17.920
switched with the Red Skull.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:24.240
While there, Cap disguises himself as a normal
guy and runs into Sam Wilson, a Harlem bird-enthusiast

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.560
who trains with Cap to become The Falcon.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.100
Together, the two of them foil the Red Skull's
plot, Cap gets his body back, and Sam Wilson

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.820
embarks on a long crime-fighting career as
The Falcon.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.840
Captain America and the Captain Americas

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:47.680
Steve Rogers might be the most famous Captain
America, but he's far from the only one.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:52.180
After becoming disillusioned with America
and donning the new identity of Nomad, Steve

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:58.160
Rogers left the Captain America identity available,
and a trio of schmoes tried out for the job.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:03.209
While the first two bailed after realizing
just how perilous the job was, Roscoe Simons

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.650
received training from The Falcon himself.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:09.670
Unfortunately, he met a gruesome end at the
hands of the Red Skull, forcing Rogers to

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:13.160
return as Captain America to end the Skull's
murderous wrath.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:18.800
Later, wracked by fear of compromising his
principles, he surrendered the official Captain

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.760
America role to John Walker, the Super-Patriot,
in favor of operating independently as "The

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:24.280
Captain."

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.960
Walker even kept Cap's shield during this
time, while Rogers used a replica.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:33.220
John Walker would later become U.S. Agent
once Steve Rogers returned to reclaim the

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.680
shield and trademark identity.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.240
In addition, both Bucky Barnes and Sam Wilson
have likewise taken over the mantle, but Rogers

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:44.400
has always found his way back from death,
retirement, or even just comic book limbo.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.020
"You just don't know when to give up, do ya?"

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.020
"I could do this all day."

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.320
Will the real Cap please stand up?

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:02.550
In 2016, Marvel used the relaunch title Captain
America: Steve Rogers to introduce the most

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:06.180
insanely controversial plot twist in a history
full of them.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:12.080
At the issue's end, Cap suddenly murders his
sidekick Jack Flag before uttering two words

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.840
which had Cap fans the world over reeling
in shock: "Hail HYDRA."

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:23.280
It turned out that Rogers had been reconstructed
by a sentient version of the Cosmic Cube after

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.960
a near-death experience.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.650
Unfortunately, the Red Skull had indoctrinated
the Cube in one of the many times that he

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:35.430
had wielded it, causing Rogers' entire history
to be retconned as a HYDRA secret agent.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:39.710
As you might expect, the real Captain America
managed to escape his imprisonment inside

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:43.480
the Cube's memory, and promptly defeated his
fascist counterpart.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.160
What makes a shield?

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:50.080
"When Captain America throws his mighty shield,
all those who chose to oppose his shield must

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:55.030
yield!"

00:05:55.030 --> 00:06:00.400
Cap's shield is perhaps the most iconic tool
in any superhero's arsenal; not only is it

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.389
virtually indestructible, it can absorb vast
amounts of kinetic energy, which is why it

00:06:05.389 --> 00:06:09.640
can withstand blows from the Hulk or Thor
without simply sending Cap flying.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:15.560
In its original comic incarnation, the shield
isn't pure vibranium, but an alloy accidentally

00:06:15.569 --> 00:06:20.449
created by scientist Myron Maclain in a metallurgical
accident.

00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:24.960
Maclain was tasked by the U.S. government
with the creation of a super-strong metal

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:30.599
to be used in tanks, and one night while laboring
over the project, Maclain fell asleep.

00:06:30.599 --> 00:06:35.449
He awoke to find that the materials he'd been
working with had mysteriously bonded, and

00:06:35.449 --> 00:06:39.060
cast the new substance using a disc-shaped
mold for tank hatches.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.839
The government gifted Cap with the shield
after he'd proven his mettle in the field,

00:06:43.839 --> 00:06:47.539
and Maclain was never able to duplicate the
process which created it.

00:06:47.539 --> 00:06:51.619
Although repeated attempts would years later
result in the creation of adamantium, the

00:06:51.619 --> 00:06:54.639
famously strong metal of which Wolverine's
skeleton is made.

00:06:54.639 --> 00:06:58.930
Replicas of Cap's shield would be employed
for various purposes over the years, but Cap

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:00.900
always preferred the real deal.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.500
Like the super-soldier himself, it's one of
a kind.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.200
Shield-throwing sense

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:10.120
It's a common misconception that Cap's shield
must be equipped with some kind of technology

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.699
to make it return to him like a boomerang,
but this isn't the case.

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:18.249
The shield's ability to absorb and disperse
kinetic energy means that once bounced off

00:07:18.249 --> 00:07:22.969
an object, it will basically keep bouncing
forever until caught, and Cap's understanding

00:07:22.969 --> 00:07:27.240
of its properties enable him to predict its
flight path with unerring accuracy.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:32.440
Handling it properly is practically a superpower
unto itself, to the point where even the two

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:37.200
most accomplished heroes to take up Cap's
mantle — Sam Wilson and Bucky Barnes — both

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.400
struggled mightily to learn how to use it
as an offensive weapon, even with extensive

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.000
training from Rogers.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:48.460
However, there is one Avenger for whom handling
the shield wasn't much of a problem: Clint

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:53.240
Barton, Hawkeye, perhaps the only hero whose
mastery of physics and ballistics is on par

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.060
with Cap's.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:58.800
Leave it to the most underrated Avenger to
measure up to Cap's exacting standards.

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:00.320
"Cap!"

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:06.180
Thanks for watching!

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.860
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.280
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

